<section xml:id="secA3-FinalReview" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Final Review</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            The final exam will be cumulative, which means it is a fair shot for me to ask anything we covered this term on the final exam (minus the abstract vector space thing we did on Tuesday). 
            Below are the sections we covered after the second midterm exam: 
            <ul marker="">
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <xref ref="sec53-Orthogonality"/> Orthogonality
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <xref ref="sec54-Rank"/> Rank of a Matrix
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <xref ref="sec33-EigenvaluesEigenvectors"/> Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <xref ref="sec34-Diagonalization"/> Diagonalization
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
            On this page, you will find review problems that addresses the aforementioned sections. 
            The final answers are included for each problem.
            Make sure you spend some time working through these problems and understanding how things work! 
        </p>

        <p>
            <alert>Note:</alert> For review problems that address material covered in the first two midterm exams, please refer to the <xref ref="secA1-Midterm1Review"/> Midterm #1 Review and <xref ref="secA2-Midterm2Review"/> Midterm #2 Review. 
        </p>

        <p>
            <alert>A quick reminder:</alert> The last class session on Thursday, June 4, will be a review session. 
            Usually this will be a work day for you to work through some problems together to prepare for the exam. 
            This is also a great opportunity for you to ask questions about anything that is not clear to you! 
        </p>
        
    </introduction>

    <worksheet xml:id="ws-Orthogonality">
        <title>Orthogonality</title>

        <exercise>
            <p>
                Let <m>\v{v}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -2 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>, <m>\v{v}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \\ -3 \end{bmatrix}</m>, and <m>\v{v}_3 = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 0 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>. 
                Determine whether this set <m>\set{\v{v}_1, \v{v}_2, \v{v}_3}</m> an orthogonal set. 
                Why or why not? 
            </p>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    Nope
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <p>
                Let <m>\v{v}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>, <m>\v{v}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} -2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>, and <m>\v{v}_3 = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix}</m>. 
                Is this set <m>\set{\v{v}_1, \v{v}_2, \v{v}_3}</m> an orthogonal set? 
                Why or why not? 
            </p>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    Nope
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <p>
                Let <m>\v{v}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>, <m>\v{v}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>, and <m>\v{v}_3 = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ -1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>. 
                Is this set <m>\set{\v{v}_1, \v{v}_2, \v{v}_3}</m> an orthogonal set? 
                Why or why not? 
            </p>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    Yep
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <p>
                Let <m>\v{v}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 3 \\ 6 \end{bmatrix}</m>, <m>\v{v}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ -6 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>, and <m>\v{v}_3 = \begin{bmatrix} 6 \\ 2 \\ -3 \end{bmatrix}</m>. 
                Is this set <m>\set{\v{v}_1, \v{v}_2, \v{v}_3}</m> an orthogonal set? 
                Why or why not? 
            </p>

            <p>
                Is this set <m>\set{\v{v}_1, \v{v}_2, \v{v}_3}</m> an orthonormal set? 
                If not, normalize these vectors to create an orthonormal set?
            </p>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    Yep to the orthogonal set and nope to the orthonormal set. 
                </p>

                <p>
                    The normalized version is <m>\left\{\begin{bmatrix} \frac{2}{7} \\ \frac{3}{7} \\ \frac{6}{7} \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} \frac{3}{7} \\ -\frac{6}{7} \\ \frac{2}{7} \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} \frac{6}{7} \\ \frac{2}{7} \\ -\frac{3}{7} \end{bmatrix}\right\}</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
        
    </worksheet>

    <worksheet xml:id="ws-RankNullity">
        <title>Column Space &amp; Null Space</title>

        <exercise>
            <p>
                Let <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -2 \amp 3 \\ -2 \amp 4 \amp -6 \\ 3 \amp -6 \amp 9 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ -1 \amp 2 \amp -3 \end{bmatrix}</m>. 
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Find the dimension of <m>\Col A</m>. 
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Find the dimension of <m>\Nul A</m>. 
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>\dim \Col A = 1</m> and <m>\dim \Nul A = 2</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <p>
                Let <m>B = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\ 2 \amp 0 \amp 5 \amp 2 \\ 1 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>. 
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Find the dimension of <m>\Col B</m>. 
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Find the dimension of <m>\Nul B</m>. 
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>\dim \Col A = 4</m> and <m>\dim \Nul A = 0</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
        
        <exercise>
            <p>
                Let <m>A</m> be an <m>n \times n</m> <em>invertible</em> matrix. 
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            What is the dimension of <m>\Col A</m>? 
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            What is the dimension of <m>\Nul A</m>? 
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>\dim \Col A = n</m> and <m>\dim \Nul A = 0</m>
                </p>

                <p>
                    <em>P.S:</em> the matrix in the previous exercise is invertible, so you see that every column has a pivot position (and hence <m>\dim \Col A = n</m>). 
                    By the Rank Nullity Theorem, <m>\dim \Nul A = 0</m>. 
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
    </worksheet>

    <worksheet xml:id="ws-FinalReview-EigenStuff">
        <title>Eigen-related Stuff</title>

        <exercise>
            <p>
                Let <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 5 \amp -1 \\ 1 \amp 3 \end{bmatrix}</m>. 
                Find <em>all</em> the eigenvalues of this matrix, and determine the corresponding eigenspace.
            </p>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    The eigenvalue is <m>\lambda = 4</m>, with the multiplicity of <m>2</m>. 
                </p>

                <p>
                    Its corresponding eigenspace is <m>E_4 = \span \left\{\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}\right\}</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <p>
                Let <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp -2 \amp -1 \\ -1 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\ -1 \amp -2 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>. 
                Find <em>all</em> the eigenvalues of this matrix. 
            </p>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    The eigenvalues are <m>\lambda = 3</m> (with the multiplicity of <m>2</m>) and <m>\lambda = -1</m> (with the multiplicity of <m>1</m>)
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <p>
                Diagonalize the matrix <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 4 \amp -5 \\ -3 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix}</m> if possible. 
                If not, state why.
            </p>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    Yep, <m>A</m> is diagonalizable. 
                </p>

                <p>
                    <m>D = \begin{bmatrix} 7 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}</m> and <m>P = \begin{bmatrix} 5 \amp 1 \\ -3 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <p>
                Diagonalize the matrix <m>B = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \\ -2 \amp 3 \amp 1 \\ -2 \amp 2 \amp 4 \end{bmatrix}</m> if possible. 
                If not, state why. 
            </p>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    Yep <m>B</m> is diagonalizable. 
                </p>

                <p>
                    <m>D = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 3 \end{bmatrix}</m> and <m>P = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <p>
                Diagonalize the matrix <m>C = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp -6 \amp -4 \\ 5 \amp -11 \amp -6 \\ -6 \amp 9 \amp 4 \end{bmatrix}</m> if possible. 
                If not, state why. 
            </p>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    Nope <m>C</m> is not diagonalizable. 
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
    </worksheet>

</section>